2. Explain in Detail about API

-> What is API?
       API (Application Programming Interface) is a medium that allows two software applications to communicate with each other.
       It works like a messenger that takes request from one system and gives response from another system.

Real Life Example
When you use a food delivery app:
You select restaurant
App sends request to restaurant server
Server sends menu details back
That communication happens through API.

Technical Example
When you login to an application:
You enter username & password
Frontend sends request to backend API
API verifies credentials from database
API sends response: Success / Failure

->Types of API
        ->REST API
Most commonly used
Uses HTTP methods
Lightweight
Uses JSON format

        ->SOAP API
Uses XML format
More secure
Mostly used in banking systems

      ->Common HTTP Methods in API
Method	Purpose
GET	Retrieve data
POST	Create new data
PUT	Update existing data
DELETE	Delete data

        ->API Testing
API Testing is done to check:
Response status code (200, 400, 500)
Response time
Data correctness
Security
Error handling

Example Test Cases:
Verify login API with valid credentials → Expect 200 OK
Verify login API with wrong password → Expect 401 Unauthorized
Verify API response time is less than 2 seconds
Verify API does not expose sensitive data

Tools Used for API Testing
Postman
Swagger
JMeter

Advantages of API
Faster communication
Reusable
Secure (with authentication)
Enables integration between systems
